  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/401c0b3fea9c98884cf8678bc33ff6442a66f3e766ba89c26917a172b21cfbac.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $4$ units to the left to give $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x+4}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-4,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/c1487a47526f4c9f546edb8911ed174bdebe80952ee226b0d59f7ac78a122c18.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x+4}.$ The point $(-4,1)$ moves to $(-4,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/520a39616c45b8c7fbe389057015d47b7f2270960b426614606596043451ce8d.png)",
